# Database Replication - Quick Revision Notes 🚀

> Goal: Keep multiple copies (replicas) of a database to improve reliability, scalability, and availability.

---

# 1. What is Database Replication?

Database Replication = Keeping multiple copies of the same database synchronized.

```
             Client
                |
        ----------------
        |              |
      Read          Write
        |              |
        v              v
       Replica ---> Primary
           ^            |
           |____________|
          Replication
```

Whenever data changes in the Primary Database, those changes are copied to one or more Replicas.

---

# Why do we need Replication?

## 1. Data Durability

If one database crashes, another copy already exists.

Without Replication

```
Database
   |
Crash ❌
   |
Data Lost
```

With Replication

```
Primary -----> Replica 1
     \
      -------> Replica 2

Primary crashes ❌

Replica becomes new database ✅
```

Benefit

- No data loss (depending on replication mode)
- High Availability
- Disaster Recovery

---

## 2. Higher Throughput

Thousands or millions of users may read data simultaneously.

Instead of sending every request to one database:

```
               Clients
                  |
      -------------------------
      |           |           |
      v           v           v
   Replica1   Replica2   Replica3
        \        |        /
          \      |      /
             Primary
```

### Common Strategy

Writes

```
Client
   |
Primary
```

Reads

```
Client
   |
Replica
```

This is called

> Read Scaling

Advantages

- Less load on Primary
- More concurrent users
- Faster read performance

---

## 3. Geo Optimization

Suppose users exist worldwide.

Without Replication

```
India User --------> USA Database
```

Every request travels across the world.

Latency ↑

With Replication

```
India User ---> India Replica

Europe User ---> Europe Replica

USA User ---> USA Primary
```

Benefits

- Lower latency
- Faster response
- Better user experience

---

# Replication Types

There are mainly two types.

---

# 1. Synchronous Replication

## Flow

```
Client
   |
Write Request
   |
Primary
   |
Copies data to ALL Replicas
   |
Wait...
   |
Replica 1 ✓

Replica 2 ✓

Replica 3 ✓
   |
Only then
   |
Success returned to Client
```

Primary waits for every replica.

Only after every replica confirms,

Client receives Success.

---

## Example

```
INSERT User

Primary writes.

↓

Replica A writes.

↓

Replica B writes.

↓

Replica C writes.

↓

Client gets Success.
```

---

## Properties

✅ Strong Consistency

Every database has exactly same data.

No stale reads.

---

## Advantages

- No inconsistent data
- Excellent durability
- Safe for banking systems
- No stale replicas

---

## Disadvantages

Primary has to wait.

```
Slow Replica

↓

Everything waits
```

Higher latency

Lower throughput

---

## Use Cases

```
Banking

Payment Systems

Financial Transactions

Critical Data
```

---

# 2. Asynchronous Replication

Primary does NOT wait.

Flow

```
Client
   |
Write
   |
Primary
   |
Immediately
Success ✅
   |
Background Replication
   |
Replica Updates Later
```

---

Example

```
INSERT User

↓

Primary stores

↓

Client gets Success

↓

2 seconds later

↓

Replica updated
```

---

## Properties

Eventual Consistency

Eventually every replica becomes identical.

Not immediately.

---

Example

Time = 0

```
Primary

Name = John
```

Replica

```
Name = Jack
```

Few seconds later

```
Primary

John

Replica

John
```

Eventually same.

---

Advantages

- Very fast writes
- Better throughput
- Better scalability
- Used by most web applications

---

Disadvantages

Temporary stale data.

If Primary crashes before replication,

latest data may be lost.

---

Common Use Cases

```
Instagram

Facebook

YouTube

Twitter

Netflix

Amazon
```

Almost every large-scale application.

---

# Synchronous vs Asynchronous

| Feature | Synchronous | Asynchronous |
|----------|------------|--------------|
| Write Speed | Slow | Fast |
| Latency | High | Low |
| Consistency | Strong | Eventual |
| Data Loss Risk | Very Low | Possible |
| Read Freshness | Always Latest | Can be Stale |
| Scalability | Lower | Higher |
| Used In | Banking | Social Media |

