# Replication Methods

After deciding **WHEN** to replicate (Sync/Async),

we decide

**WHAT** to replicate.

Three methods.

---

# 1. Statement-Based Replication

Primary sends SQL query itself.

Example

```
INSERT INTO users
VALUES (1,'Anubhav');
```

Replica executes same SQL.

```
Primary

INSERT ...

↓

Replica executes INSERT
```

---

Problem

Non-deterministic queries.

Example

```
INSERT INTO logs
VALUES(now());
```

Primary

```
10:00:00
```

Replica executes later

```
10:00:05
```

Different values.

Data mismatch.

---

Another Example

```
RAND()

UUID()

CURRENT_TIMESTAMP

NOW()
```

All can produce different outputs.

---

Advantages

- Small replication logs
- Easy implementation

---

Disadvantages

- Can create inconsistent data
- Unsafe for non-deterministic queries

---

# 2. WAL (Write Ahead Log) Replication

Instead of SQL,

copy disk changes.

Primary stores changes inside WAL.

Replica replays those changes.

```
Application

↓

Primary

↓

WAL

↓

Replica replays WAL

↓

Same data
```

---

Think Like This

Instead of saying

```
INSERT User
```

WAL says

```
Byte 200

↓

Change

↓

Byte 450

↓

Change
```

Very low-level.

---

Advantages

- Exact copy
- Very reliable
- Very fast
- No SQL ambiguity

---

Disadvantages

Database Specific.

Example

```
MySQL WAL

↓

Cannot be directly used by PostgreSQL
```

Every database engine stores logs differently.

---

Used In

```
PostgreSQL

MySQL

Oracle

SQL Server
```

Each has its own internal log format.

---

# 3. Logical Replication (Replication Log)

Modern approach.

Instead of SQL

Instead of Bytes

Replicate actual row changes.

Example

```
Row ID = 12

Name

John

↓

Updated

↓

Johnny
```

Replica simply updates Row 12.

```
Update Row 12

Name = Johnny
```

No SQL execution.

No byte copying.

Only logical changes.

---

Advantages

- Database Agnostic
- Easier migration
- Easier debugging
- Supports filtering tables
- Supports cross-database replication

---

Example

```
MySQL

↓

Logical Changes

↓

Kafka

↓

PostgreSQL
```

Possible because only logical row events are transferred.

---

# Comparison of Replication Methods

| Method | Copies | Problem | Best For |
|---------|--------|---------|----------|
| Statement Based | SQL Queries | Non-deterministic SQL | Small systems |
| WAL | Physical bytes | DB specific | Same database engine |
| Logical Replication | Row changes | Slightly larger logs | Modern distributed systems |

